        title: 'The Coat',
        body: 'Jonah kept the coat because those were the terms and the terms had been said out loud in front of a witness, which is more than most arrangements get. Eli had needed money in the eighth month, before the harvest came in, and he had nothing to put up. He offered his coat. Jonah said that was fine and wrote the date. It was not a cruel loan. Jonah did not add much — a little, for the trouble and the waiting, which is what anybody would do and what his own father had done. The coat went on a peg in Jonah’s back room and he stopped seeing it after the first week, the way you stop seeing a thing that lives on a peg. In the second month Eli came by and did not ask for the coat, because he had agreed, and a man who has agreed does not ask. He stood in the doorway a while with his arms crossed over his chest and talked about the weather and then he went home. Jonah told his wife afterward that Eli had seemed cold to him lately, meaning distant. She looked at the peg and then at her husband and did not say anything for a moment. Then she asked him where he thought Eli had slept last night. Jonah said he had not thought about it. She said that was the whole thing, that he had not thought about it, and that Yahweh had written the question down in advance because He knew nobody would think about it. Jonah took the coat off the peg that evening and walked it over and did not make a speech about it. He has not taken a pledge off a poor man since, and he will tell you it was not the sermon that did it. It was standing in his own back room understanding that there was a man three streets away who had been cold for two months while a coat hung on a peg he had stopped seeing.',
