        title: 'What My Father Did, and What I Stopped Doing',
        body: 'My father ran money out to men in our neighbourhood for thirty years and he was not a loan shark and I will not let anybody call him one. He was the only credit some of those men could get, and he knew every one of their names and most of their mothers. He also charged them, and the ones who could least afford it paid the most, because that is how risk works and he would have explained it to you exactly that way. I took over the book when he passed. I was forty-four and I had never once questioned any of it, because it was simply the water. Then a man in our church taught Ezekiel 18 on a Wednesday and got to verse 13, and he did not soften it, and I sat there doing arithmetic about my own ledger. But he kept reading, and that is the part I need people to hear, because if he had stopped at 13 I think I would have gone home defeated and changed nothing. He read 17. The son who takes his hand off the poor, who has not received usury nor increase — he shall not die for the iniquity of his father, he shall surely live. My father’s book was not my sentence. It was my decision. It took me nineteen months to unwind that ledger and it cost me more than I am going to put in writing, and four of those men are still with me on different terms entirely, and two of them I simply released and never told anybody. I am not writing this to say I did something admirable. I am writing it because a man my age reading Ezekiel 18 needs to know the chapter does not end where it hurts. There is a verse after that one and it is a door.',
