    lesson: 'Yahweh states a rule about interest early, and He states it before most of what He says about money, which is itself worth noticing. It is not a cap, and it is not a guideline. It is a prohibition, and it names its borrower in the same breath. "If thou lend money to any of my people that is poor by thee, thou shalt not be to him as an usurer, neither shalt thou lay upon him usury." (Exodus 22:25) Before discussing rates at all, find who the sentence is about. Two qualifiers sit inside it and both are load-bearing: MY PEOPLE, and POOR BY THEE. Not lending in general. Not borrowers in general. A particular man, in a particular condition, near you. Then let the following verses fix the register, because they are what keep this from floating off into abstraction. "If thou at all take thy neighbour’s raiment to pledge, thou shalt deliver it unto him by that the sun goeth down:" (Exodus 22:26) What the borrower puts up so the lender can take it if he does not pay is called his collateral, and the collateral under discussion here is a garment. Sit with that. The man has nothing else to pledge — no vehicle, no second property, no instrument. His coat. And the pledge cannot even be held overnight. And then Yahweh explains His own reasoning, which He is under no obligation to do and frequently does not: "For that is his covering only, it is his raiment for his skin: wherein shall he sleep? and it shall come to pass, when he crieth unto me, that I will hear; for I am gracious." (Exodus 22:27) WHEREIN SHALL HE SLEEP. Read it twice. The question is not rhetorical decoration; it is the actual condition of an actual man tonight. And then the clause no lender should ever be able to forget: when he crieth unto me, that I will hear. The borrower has a direct line, it is open, and it does not require the lender’s permission. Now the limit, handled honestly in both directions, because this is the passage most often stretched and most often explained away, and both moves are failures. What the text unambiguously forbids is taking increase from a poor brother, and it does so in the strongest register Scripture possesses. What the text does not do is set out a doctrine of capital markets, mortgage lending, commercial credit, or the deposit rate on a savings account. Where the Word is reticent we stay with what it says and we stop; we do not manufacture a position to fill the silence and then attach His authority to it. That restraint is not a weakening of the rule. It is what keeps the rule from being dismissed, because a reader who catches you stretching one verse will discount the next twelve. Then see how heavily Scripture itself weights this, which is what keeps a narrow reading from becoming a small one. Psalm 15 opens by asking who shall abide in His tabernacle and answers with a list of what such a man is like, and this is on it: "He that putteth not out his money to usury, nor taketh reward against the innocent. He that doeth these things shall never be moved." (Psalms 15:5) It stands beside taking a bribe against an innocent man. That is the company this sin keeps. Ezekiel 18 then makes it a marker of life and death, traced deliberately across three generations. The just man is described in part by what he has not done: "He that hath not given forth upon usury, neither hath taken any increase, that hath withdrawn his hand from iniquity, hath executed true judgment between man and man," (Ezekiel 18:8). The man who does it is condemned in the hardest words the chapter has: "Hath given forth upon usury, and hath taken increase: shall he then live? he shall not live: he hath done all these abominations; he shall surely die; his blood shall be upon him." (Ezekiel 18:13) And then comes the reason to teach the whole chapter rather than the one verse that stings, because the chapter has a door in it. The son who turns from his father’s practice is not held to his father’s account: "That hath taken off his hand from the poor, that hath not received usury nor increase, hath executed my judgments, hath walked in my statutes; he shall not die for the iniquity of his father, he shall surely live." (Ezekiel 18:17) TAKEN OFF HIS HAND FROM THE POOR. A man reading this who has been doing it, or who inherited a book of it, is not reading his sentence. He is reading his instruction. So the work is two columns and one question. On the left, go and find the published rate your own institution charges the weakest borrower it will lend to. Not your rate — theirs. It is published, it takes about four minutes, and almost nobody who banks there has ever looked at it. On the right, write down every loan you personally have outstanding inside your own house, your church or your family, with its actual terms, including the ones that were never written down. Then put the question this session exists to ask, and answer it out loud rather than in your head: is there anybody in either column who is poor by thee?',
