        kind: 'parable', tone: 'solemn',
        title: 'The Field That Bought the Groceries',
        body: 'The Ramírez family did not make a bad decision. That is what makes this worth telling. In a thin year they borrowed against forty acres to cover the season, because the alternative was to sell the forty acres outright and they were not going to do that, and everybody they asked agreed it was the sounder move. The loan was ordinary. The rate was ordinary. Nobody in the story behaves badly at any point. The next year was also thin, and the payment on the first loan was now part of what made it thin, which is a sentence that is easy to write and takes about nine months to feel. They borrowed again, on the same ground, because it was the only thing they had that a lender would look at. By the fourth year the land was carrying two obligations and producing for one, and the difference was being covered by the part of the harvest they used to keep. Ana was the one who said it out loud, at the kitchen table, in the fifth spring. She said: the field is buying the groceries now. Her husband asked what she meant, and she said it plainly. The field used to grow the groceries. Now it is collateral for buying them at the store, and the day it stops being worth more than we owe on it, we do not have a field or a store. They did not lose the farm. They sold eleven acres that autumn, deliberately, while the sale was still their decision to make, and they hated it and they did it, and the remaining twenty-nine are still theirs. Ana tells younger couples one thing, and it is not about rates. She tells them the second rung is the one that matters, and you can feel it under your foot if you are paying attention: you are on it the day the thing that produces starts securing the thing that gets eaten.',
