        title: 'What It Cost Me to Go First',
        body: 'I was a deacon and I held paper on four families in our own church, and I did not think of myself as a lender. I thought of myself as the guy who helps. I charged them, though. Not much by the standards of what they could have got anywhere else, and every one of them thanked me, and two of them are still friends. Our pastor taught Nehemiah 5 on a Sunday night and I sat through it waiting for it to be about somebody else, and it kept not being. What got me was not verse 7, where he rebukes the nobles. I could hear that one as being about other men. It was verse 10. I likewise, and my brethren, and my servants, might exact of them money and corn. He was doing it. He said so first, out loud, in front of the assembly, before he asked one other man to stop. I sat there understanding that I had been prepared to agree with the sermon and not prepared to say that sentence. I said it two weeks later at a men’s breakfast and it was the hardest thirty seconds of my life in that church. I told them what I held, and from whom, and what I had charged, and that I was releasing the balances that week. One man got angry with me, which I did not expect, and told me I was making the rest of them look bad. He was not entirely wrong. Three of the four families are still in the church. The fourth moved to Georgia and sends a card at Christmas. What I would say to any man reading this who is in it: the rebuke is survivable and going second is not. Nehemiah went first. That is the whole reason anybody else moved.',
