        title: 'In the Presence of His Friend',
        body: 'Desmond had already decided. He and Renee had talked about it in the spring, sitting on their own back steps, and they had agreed: they would help people with money they could afford to give away, and they would not put their name on anybody else’s loan. It was not a hard conversation. They both thought it was obvious. In October his brother-in-law came by with a folder and a man from the dealership on speakerphone, and the truck was for the business, and the business had two contracts already signed, and everybody in the room was in a good mood. The man on the phone said they just needed a second name on the line because of one thing on the credit file from four years ago that was not even really his fault. Desmond said he would need to think about it. And his brother-in-law did not argue, which was worse. He just looked at him, in Desmond’s own kitchen, with his sister standing at the counter, and said okay, that is fine, in the voice of a man who had been told no. Desmond signed eleven minutes later. Nothing bad happened for two years. Then the second contract fell through and the truck payment came to Desmond’s house, and it came as a total balance rather than a payment, because that is what the second line means. He is not angry at his brother-in-law, who tried, and who is still family and still comes at Christmas. What he says now, to men who ask him about it, is one sentence: I made the right decision in April on my back steps and I made a different one in October in my own kitchen, and the difference was not the truck. It was that in October there was somebody standing there.',
