    lesson: 'A great many believers have spent their lives collapsing two different faults into one, and the collapse does real damage in both directions. Some have never built a store at all, because they have heard hoarding preached against and could not find the line, so they treated owning nothing as the safer form of obedience. Others have accumulated steadily for decades and called it prudence without once asking what it is for. Scripture does not collapse them, and the cleanest place to see that is a verse most people read too quickly. "There is treasure to be desired and oil in the dwelling of the wise; but a foolish man spendeth it up." (Proverbs 21:20) Before going further, answer who is called foolish here, because the instinctive answer is wrong. The fool is NOT the man with the store. The store is located, plainly and without apology, in the dwelling of the WISE. The fool is the one who SPENDETH IT UP. And then note the contents, which is the detail that stops this becoming a simple defence of accumulation: treasure AND oil. Oil is a consumable. It is burned. It is not a trophy, not an heirloom, not a number on a page. The store Scripture commends already contains things intended to be used up, which tells you from the outset that a store was never meant merely to sit. Then the teacher He sends you to, and it is not a person: "Go to the ant, thou sluggard; consider her ways, and be wise:" (Proverbs 6:6) And immediately the qualifier that turns this from a lesson about industry into a lesson about the interior life: "Which having no guide, overseer, or ruler," (Proverbs 6:7) Three separate words for external authority, all of them negated. No one is making her. There is no supervisor, no deadline, no inspection, no consequence administered by anybody at all. That is precisely your own position with respect to your own savings: nobody is checking, nothing is due, and no one will ever call you about it. And she does it anyway, which is the whole reason Solomon sends you to look at her. Then the timing, which is the practical heart of this session and the thing most people have backwards for their entire lives: "Provideth her meat in the summer, and gathereth her food in the harvest." (Proverbs 6:8) IN THE SUMMER. Slow down here. Summer is when nothing is wrong. Summer is when there is no pressure, no urgency, no motivating fear and no obvious reason to do anything. And it is the ONLY time a store can be built, because in winter you are not building a store — you are eating one. A household that intends to begin saving once things get a little tighter has described an impossibility and does not know it. Then Solomon returns to the same small creature and adds something for anyone who long ago concluded that none of this applied to them because they never earned enough: "There be four things which are little upon the earth, but they are exceeding wise:" (Proverbs 30:24) Little, and exceeding wise, and the two are not in tension. And then: "The ants are a people not strong, yet they prepare their meat in the summer;" (Proverbs 30:25) NOT STRONG, YET THEY PREPARE. Strength is not the faculty that builds a store. Preparation is, and preparation is fully available to somebody with very little — which is why the example Yahweh chose is an insect and not a lion. Now the distinction this session promised, handled with restraint, because these passages commend preparation and do not set out a doctrine of hoarding, and manufacturing one in order to fill the silence is exactly the failure this house refuses. What the texts DO show is consistent across all three. The ant’s store exists against a named season, and winter comes, and it is eaten. Joseph’s storehouses in the first session were OPENED. And the wise dwelling holds oil, which is burned. Every store Scripture commends has a release in view. So the question to put to your own store is not how large it is. The question is what it is FOR. A store with a named purpose is saving. A store whose owner cannot picture ever opening it is a different thing, and these verses do not need to say another word for a man to feel that difference in his own chest. One honest note about the modern picture, because this house states what is established plainly and refuses what it cannot source. That a great many households cannot meet a moderate unexpected expense without borrowing is well documented and it is not in serious dispute — that reality is why this course exists at all. This course will NOT quote a specific percentage for it, because those figures circulate detached from the surveys that produced them, and a true thing is not improved by a number nobody can trace. The condition is real; the statistic is not ours to repeat. So the work is two numbers and one sentence, and the third is the one that matters. FIRST: how many weeks your household could run on what you could actually REACH this week without borrowing. Reached, not owned — a retirement account you would be penalised to touch is a different instrument and it is not this number. SECOND: the date you last added anything to it, which will be more informative than the balance. THIRD: one sentence naming the season your store exists against. Most people have never written the third one, and it is the sentence that tells you, without anybody having to judge you, which of these two things you have been doing all along.',
